# Git converts no file's line endings, on any system. A checkout on Windows, where Git for Windows sets
# core.autocrlf=true, then holds the same bytes as one on Linux: `design --check` compares generated
# files byte for byte, library/sources.csv names each maker's file by its SHA256, and bash scripts stop
# at a carriage return. Not `text=auto eol=lf`: it would store the 15 files committed with CRLF (3 of
# them makers' models) again as LF on their next change, and their SHA256 rows would no longer hold.
* -text
# Code is the exception: Git stores it LF whatever an editor on Windows saves, and checks it out LF, so
# bash and Python on Linux never meet a carriage return. No committed .py or .sh file holds CRLF.
*.py text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
tools/workcenter text eol=lf
